<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ==============================================================================================
  AWS CardDemo - Maven module descriptor for the COBOL to Java 21 / Spring Boot 3.x migration.

  This is the ONLY build manifest in the repository. Before this module existed there was no
  automated build at all: README.md step 4 ("Compile the Programs") directs the reader to "the
  compile process followed by your mainframe shopfloor". The entire Maven build, the application
  entry class and the application configuration are therefore net-new scaffolding, created from
  nothing (AAP implicit requirement I7). There is also no aggregator pom at any level of this
  repository, which is why the <parent> below carries an EMPTY <relativePath/> - see the note
  beside it; omitting that single element is the most common way this file breaks.

  WHAT THIS MODULE IS
    A like-for-like translation of the 28 COBOL programs in app/cbl into Java - not a redesign.
    The 17 CICS online programs become stateless REST controllers, the 8 JCL-invoked programs
    plus the untriggered CBTRN01C and the standalone customer reader become Spring Batch jobs,
    and the 2 CALLed subprograms become injected Spring components. Every observable output -
    record bytes, field values, numeric scale, message text and return codes - is held identical
    to what the COBOL produces today.

  VERSION POLICY (AAP 0.5.2 - 0.5.4; binding practices B1 and B2)
    * Java 21 (LTS)  -> <release>21</release>, and nothing newer. Verified on JDK 21.0.11.
    * Apache Maven   -> 3.9 or newer is required; this build is verified with 3.9.16. The
                        distribution's 3.8.7 candidate sits below that floor and was rejected.
                        No enforcer plugin polices the floor, deliberately: enforcer is outside
                        the closed plugin set, and adding it would be scope creep (practice B4).
    * spring-boot-starter-parent 3.5.16 -> the highest published 3.x release. The 4.x parent
                        line, and the Spring Batch 6.x line that belongs to it, were both
                        evaluated and DELIBERATELY REJECTED: the build prompt pins Spring Boot
                        3.x, and constraint fidelity outranks recency (practice B2). 3.5.16 is
                        a deliberate pin, not staleness - please do not "helpfully" upgrade it.
    * Spring Batch 5.2.6, JUnit 5, Mockito, AssertJ, HikariCP, Jackson, Jakarta Validation, the
                        embedded servlet container and H2 all take their versions from the
                        parent BOM, so none of them names a <version> here. Only the three build
                        plugins named in AAP 0.5.4 carry an explicit <version>. Every version in
                        this file is a fixed, published release: there is no placeholder and no
                        moving version marker anywhere in it (gate G2, practice B1).

  CLOSED DEPENDENCY SET (AAP 0.5.6)
    Exactly six dependencies are declared below and nothing may be added to them. Every
    technology enumerated in AAP 0.5.6 is intentionally absent: no object-relational mapper, no
    schema-migration tool, no relational database driver, no authentication or credential-hashing
    library, no cloud service SDK, no metrics or tracing exporter, no container-based test
    harness, no reactive web stack, no API-documentation generator, no compile-time accessor or
    mapper generator, and no third-party COBOL copybook parser. The fixed-width record codecs are
    hand-written precisely so that every byte offset stays reviewable against its copybook
    (practice B11), and the sign-on credential comparison is preserved exactly as the COBOL
    performs it, which is why no authentication library appears here (practice B6).

  PERSISTENCE AND THE INTENTIONALLY ABSENT DRIVER (AAP 0.5.5; residual risk R-E)
    Data access is plain JDBC over the existing VSAM and sequential datasets: no DDL, no schema
    change, no new database, no generated table definitions. There is zero EXEC SQL in all 28
    programs, README.md lists relational and hierarchical database support as Roadmap items
    rather than current behaviour, and indexed VSAM has no standard published JDBC driver -
    so no driver coordinate is compiled into this build. The DataSource is bound entirely from
    src/main/resources/application.yml, keyed by the dataset names defined in
    app/csd/CARDDEMO.CSD and the batch-only DD names in app/jcl, and the site-specific mainframe
    driver is a DEPLOYMENT-TIME input. Residual risk R-E is accepted and recorded here:
    production connectivity cannot be exercised in this environment, so the repositories are
    validated against the fixture-backed parity harness instead, with H2 confined to TEST scope.

  THE GATE
    One command performs the whole cycle, non-interactively and with no watch mode:
        mvn -f app/java/pom.xml clean verify
    It compiles under Java 21, runs every unit test and all 560 parity cases with results
    streamed to stdout, and then enforces at least 90% BRANCH coverage - at bundle level AND
    independently per package, so that no package can hide behind another package's coverage
    (gate G49). BRANCH rather than LINE is the counter that matters here: the migration is judged
    on 508 COBOL 88-level condition names, 111 EVALUATE statements and the guard chains that
    surround them.

  This build reads and writes only inside app/java. The reference trees app/cbl, app/cpy,
  app/cpy-bms, app/bms, app/jcl, app/proc, app/csd, app/ctl, app/catlg and app/data are the sole
  oracle for behavioural equivalence, and no plugin declared here reads from, generates into or
  rewrites any of them (practice B3, gate G5).
  ==============================================================================================
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!-- The empty <relativePath/> is MANDATORY and must not be removed. This repository contains
         no aggregator pom at any level (app/pom.xml does not exist and neither does one at the
         root), so app/java is a standalone module. Without the empty element Maven first looks
         for ../pom.xml, fails to find it, and the parent-resolution failure then surfaces
         confusingly as a cascade of "managed version is missing" errors. The empty element tells
         Maven to skip the filesystem search and resolve the parent from the repository. -->
    <parent>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
        <version>3.5.16</version>
        <relativePath/>
    </parent>

    <!-- groupId and artifactId derive from the mandated root package com.vsergeychik.carddemo.
         Version 1.0.0 aligns with the source version footer "Ver: CardDemo_v1.0-15-g27d6c6f-68"
         carried by app/cbl/CSUTLDTC.cbl, app/jcl/INTCALC.jcl and app/jcl/POSTTRAN.jcl. It is a
         fixed release identifier by design (practice B1). -->
    <groupId>com.vsergeychik</groupId>
    <artifactId>carddemo</artifactId>
    <version>1.0.0</version>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <name>carddemo</name>
    <description>
        AWS CardDemo - like-for-like migration of 28 COBOL programs to Java 21 and Spring Boot
        3.x: 17 CICS online transactions as stateless REST controllers, 10 JCL jobs as Spring
        Batch jobs, and 12 dataset repositories over plain JDBC with no schema change.
    </description>

    <properties>
        <!-- Java 21 (LTS). The parent defaults java.version to 17 and derives
             maven.compiler.release from it; both are overridden explicitly so the target release
             is unambiguous however the build is invoked (practice B8). -->
        <java.version>21</java.version>
        <maven.compiler.release>21</maven.compiler.release>

        <!-- Source-file encoding, stated explicitly so the build never depends on the platform
             default. This is the encoding of the *Java sources* only. It is emphatically NOT the
             mainframe *data* charset: the EBCDIC and ASCII dataset charsets are named explicitly
             at runtime by the configuration and the codec classes under src, and must never be
             conflated with, or configured through, this property (practice B8). -->
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
        <project.reporting.outputEncoding>UTF-8</project.reporting.outputEncoding>

        <!-- The three explicitly-versioned build plugins (AAP 0.5.4). Each version appears
             exactly once, here, so it can be audited in one place. Note that the parent BOM
             manages maven-compiler-plugin but NOT maven-surefire-plugin, and defines no jacoco
             property at all, so all three pins are load-bearing. -->
        <maven-compiler-plugin.version>3.15.0</maven-compiler-plugin.version>
        <maven-surefire-plugin.version>3.5.6</maven-surefire-plugin.version>
        <jacoco.version>0.8.15</jacoco.version>

        <!-- The parity project's primary quality bar: at least 90% BRANCH coverage. Declared once
             so it is auditable, and applied below at BOTH bundle and package granularity.
             DO NOT LOWER THIS VALUE - it is the mandated acceptance gate (G49). -->
        <jacoco.branch.minimum>0.90</jacoco.branch.minimum>
    </properties>

    <!--
      Exactly six dependencies, no more. Not one of them names a <version>: every version is
      managed by the spring-boot-starter-parent 3.5.16 BOM (practice B1).
    -->
    <dependencies>
        <!-- Online layer. The 17 CICS pseudo-conversational programs become stateless REST
             controllers whose request and response fields are a 1:1 projection of the 441 BMS
             field definitions. Conversation state travels in the payload, never in a server-side
             session, so a servlet web stack is all that is required. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Batch layer. The 8 programs invoked by EXEC PGM= in app/jcl, plus the untriggered
             CBTRN01C and the standalone customer file reader, become Spring Batch jobs whose step
             boundaries, DD-to-resource bindings, PARM parsing and COND gating mirror the JCL.
             This starter supplies Spring Batch 5.2.6 transitively; spring-batch-core is
             deliberately NOT declared here and must never be pinned to the 6.x line, which
             belongs to the Spring Boot 4 BOM (practice B2). -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-batch</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Data access. JdbcTemplate and a pooled DataSource for the 12 dataset repositories.
             Plain JDBC by mandate: the record layouts stay exactly as the 28 copybooks define
             them, so no mapping framework is introduced and no schema is ever created. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-jdbc</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Bean Validation for the 17 request payloads. Field lengths come from the symbolic-map
             "xxxI PIC X(n)" items, so validation is the enforcement point for screen field
             widths that CICS used to enforce for free. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Test aggregate: JUnit 5 Jupiter, Mockito, AssertJ and the Spring test support. Tests
             are a first-class deliverable authored alongside the code, not bolted on afterwards
             (practice B10): this drives the 28 parameterised parity test classes, their 560
             declarative cases and every per-package unit test. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!-- TEST SCOPE ONLY, and it must stay that way. Two jobs, both confined to the test
             lifecycle: it backs the Spring Batch JobRepository (Batch 5 requires a
             DataSource-backed repository, so a batch test context will not start without one),
             and it hosts the parity harness's seeded fixture rows. It is never promoted to
             runtime or compile scope, because production reaches the existing datasets through
             the deployment-supplied driver described in the header (AAP 0.5.5, risk R-E). -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.h2database</groupId>
            <artifactId>h2</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <!-- Stable artifact name, so the documented run command does not have to be rewritten
             whenever the module version changes. -->
        <finalName>carddemo</finalName>

        <!-- Plugin ORDER is significant: surefire is declared before jacoco so that, within the
             shared test phase, the tests run first and the coverage report is generated from the
             execution data they produced. -->
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${maven-compiler-plugin.version}</version>
                <configuration>
                    <!-- <release> rather than the <source>/<target> pair: release additionally
                         validates that only Java 21 platform APIs are referenced, which is what
                         "targets Java 21 (LTS)" actually has to mean. No preview features are
                         enabled and no annotation processor is configured, because there is no
                         compile-time code generator in the closed dependency set. -->
                    <release>21</release>
                    <!-- Retain parameter names in the bytecode so payload binding and diagnostics
                         report the real field names rather than arg0, arg1 (practice B8). -->
                    <parameters>true</parameters>
                    <compilerArgs>
                        <!-- Advisory only. Warnings are surfaced, never promoted to errors: a
                             like-for-like translation legitimately produces constructs the
                             compiler would rather see written differently, and failing the build
                             on them would pressure implementers into changing behaviour. -->
                        <arg>-Xlint:all</arg>
                    </compilerArgs>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>

            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${maven-surefire-plugin.version}</version>
                <configuration>
                    <!-- Results stream to stdout instead of report files, so a single
                         non-interactive command shows every failure inline (practice B7, gate
                         G54). There is no watch mode and nothing here waits on terminal input. -->
                    <useFile>false</useFile>
                    <!-- Full stack traces: when a parity case fails, the diff has to be traceable
                         to the exact translation decision that caused it. -->
                    <trimStackTrace>false</trimStackTrace>
                    <!-- Deterministic, filesystem-independent execution order, so a parity run is
                         reproducible across machines. -->
                    <runOrder>alphabetical</runOrder>
                    <!-- The default includes already match both the per-package *Test classes and
                         the 28 *ParityTest classes, so <includes> is intentionally left alone.
                         Narrowing it would silently drop parity coverage. -->
                </configuration>
            </plugin>

            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
                <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${jacoco.version}</version>
                <executions>
                    <!-- Instrument before the tests run. prepare-agent binds to initialize by
                         default and sets the argLine that surefire then picks up. -->
                    <execution>
                        <id>jacoco-prepare-agent</id>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>prepare-agent</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>

                    <!-- Human-readable and machine-readable reports, produced in the test phase
                         immediately after surefire. -->
                    <execution>
                        <id>jacoco-report</id>
                        <phase>test</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>report</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>

                    <!--
                      THE COVERAGE GATE. Bound to verify because "mvn clean verify" is the single
                      documented gating command (practice B7) - not to test, and not to install.

                      Two rules, deliberately, and both are required:
                        * BUNDLE  - the module as a whole must clear the bar.
                        * PACKAGE - EVERY package must clear it independently. In a single-module
                                    build a BUNDLE-only rule would let a well-covered package mask
                                    an untested one, which is exactly the outcome gate G49
                                    forbids when it says no module may hide behind another's
                                    coverage. The package rule is what actually delivers that
                                    guarantee here, across config, common, account, customer,
                                    transaction, billing, card, user, admin, statement and util.

                      The counter is BRANCH, not LINE and not INSTRUCTION: the parity surface is
                      508 88-level condition names, 111 EVALUATE statements and the guard chains
                      around them, and only branch coverage evidences that both sides of each of
                      those decisions were actually exercised.
                    -->
                    <execution>
                        <id>jacoco-check-branch-coverage</id>
                        <phase>verify</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>check</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <!-- Stated explicitly: a gate that cannot fail the build is not a
                                 gate (practice B8). -->
                            <haltOnFailure>true</haltOnFailure>
                            <rules>
                                <rule>
                                    <element>BUNDLE</element>
                                    <limits>
                                        <limit>
                                            <counter>BRANCH</counter>
                                            <value>COVEREDRATIO</value>
                                            <minimum>${jacoco.branch.minimum}</minimum>
                                        </limit>
                                    </limits>
                                </rule>
                                <rule>
                                    <element>PACKAGE</element>
                                    <limits>
                                        <limit>
                                            <counter>BRANCH</counter>
                                            <value>COVEREDRATIO</value>
                                            <minimum>${jacoco.branch.minimum}</minimum>
                                        </limit>
                                    </limits>
                                </rule>
                            </rules>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>

            <!-- Declared WITHOUT a <version>: both the version and the repackage execution come
                 from the parent's pluginManagement, so this adds no new coordinate and no fourth
                 explicit plugin version. It produces the runnable Boot artifact and backs
                 spring-boot:run. mainClass is pinned explicitly rather than left to main-class
                 scanning, which removes any ambiguity about the entry point (practice B8). -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
                <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <configuration>
                    <mainClass>com.vsergeychik.carddemo.CardDemoApplication</mainClass>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
